t and never deploys the bad client, but every delivery it is waiting on now either times out or never arrives, so its own webhook_confirmation_p99_ms (the alerting metric) and webhook_retry_queue_depth ramp starting 10:35Z -- ten minutes after webhook-dispatcher's own onset -- crossing the 4000ms alert threshold around 11:03Z and firing at 11:10Z, and its 'webhook confirmation delayed: awaiting delivery receipt past deadline' pattern (9/min from 10:35Z) ends up LOUDER over the window than webhook-dispatcher's own connect-failure pattern (5/min from 10:25Z) -- the victim outshouts the actual failing service, exactly the trap a naive 'loudest pattern wins' heuristic falls for. Two mechanism-traps deploy to webhook-dispatcher, the causally-relevant service, and both read as maximally guilty yet are exonerable purely by code semantics: (1) buffer-pool-trap ('perf(webhooks): pool request buffers', deployed 2026-07-23T09:50Z, PRE-onset) adds a sync.Pool for the byte buffer used to encode outbound JSON payloads -- a memory-shaped change that touches encodePayload, not the HTTP client or transport, and cannot open or close a socket. Its mechanism is falsified in the data itself: container_memory_rss_mb stays flat (480MB +/-3% jitter) for the entire window, including through the incident, which is exactly what a buffer-pool change touching only allocation reuse should look like, and rules out any memory-pressure-driven explanation. (2) error-wrap-trap ('refactor(webhooks): wrap dispatch errors with endpoint context', deployed 10:45Z, twenty minutes after the connect failures began) touches the exact function (Post) whose error becomes the incident's own error log line -- maximally suspicious surface match -- but the diff shows the 'webhook post failed: %w' wrapping already existed before this commit; the change only appends '(endpoint=%s)' onto an already-wrapped error for observability, and does not touch d.client, the transport, or connection handling at all. It also deploys twenty minutes AFTER tcp_new_conns_per_s had already begun ramping at onset (10:25Z) and after the connect-failure logs had already been firing for 20 minutes, so the underlying failure demonstrably predates this commit's deploy. billing-decoy (billing-api, 2026-07-23T11:06Z, 4 minutes before fired_at, and the most recent billing-api deploy before the alert) is a CSS/PDF-margin-style constant tweak on an unrelated invoice template file -- classic innocent-deploy-near-alert bait on the alerting service itself. worker-bump-decoy (partner-sync-worker, 09:15Z) is an unrelated go.mod patch bump on the uninvolved background service. The 20 deploys_auto entries (7+ pre-onset, cycling across all three services) are routine dependency/docs/CI noise touching none of go.mod's httpkit line, client.go, or dispatch.go. patterns_extra (TLS renewal, disk usage, pool warmup retries, kafka rebalancing; counts 42-66) are stale pre-existing background chatter on billing-api/partner-sync-worker, well below both incident patterns. partner-sync-worker's sync_backlog_depth spike (20 -> 300, 08:15-08:45Z) fully self-heals nearly two hours before onset and is unrelated noise on the uninvolved background service. first_failing_service is webhook-dispatcher (its own connection/handshake/dispatch metrics and connect-failure logs move first, at onset); billing-api is blast radius, waiting on deliveries that never arrive. Remediation: roll back webhook-dispatcher to the pre-2.0.0 httpkit client (or, forward-fix, construct httpkit.Config with KeepAlive explicitly enabled to match v1 defaults).
